# FPLReview's exported projections — a paid competitor's product, read at
# runtime by frontend/lib/fplreview-projections.ts and absent everywhere but
# this machine. Ignored so it can never be committed: it must not be
# redistributed, and a static import of it once made the whole site unbuildable
# from a clean checkout. See frontend/test/no-untracked-imports.test.ts.
frontend/data/fplreview-projections.json
# ...and any sync-conflict copy of it. The exact-filename rule above does not
# match "fplreview-projections 2.json", and `git add -A` duly staged one — a
# licensed competitor's export, one commit away from being redistributed.
# `no-untracked-imports.test.ts` catches conflict copies generally; this stops
# the one that must never be committed at the source.
frontend/data/fplreview-projections*.json
# Anywhere, not just in data/. `npm run build` copies the export into
# .open-next/server-functions/, and this repo tracks .open-next — so a build
# followed by `git add -A` stages the licensed file from a path nobody thinks
# to look at. Caught by no-untracked-imports.test.ts only because it happened
# to be a sync-conflict copy; this rule catches the ordinary case.
**/fplreview-projections*.json

# Build output. Regenerated by `npm run build` and `npm run build:cloudflare`,
# read from disk by wrangler, and deployed from by nothing — no workflow
# references any of it.
#
# It was never a decision to track it: 411 files arrived in commit 0f98e67,
# whose subject is "feat: remove email" and whose diff was more than half build
# output. The tracked copy then went stale, so every source change carried
# dozens of unrelated artifact diffs, and `.open-next/server-functions/` is the
# path that nearly took a licensed FPLReview export into a commit.
#
# The blobs stay in history; this only stops new ones accumulating.
frontend/.open-next/
frontend/.sites-bundle/
frontend/.openai/
frontend/.wrangler/
frontend/dist/

# Browser-automation output from verification runs.
.playwright-mcp/
